The exp(x) function is a shortcut for a very common mathematical calculation: taking Euler's number (e) and raising it to the power of x.
# e is a special number in math (about 2.718).
# x is the number you want to use as the exponent.
So, math.exp(2) is just a quick and accurate way of writing e2, which is about 7.389.

The main purpose of this function is to easily work with things that grow or decay exponentially, 
which is why the example mentions population growth.

import math
print(math.exp(2))        # e² ≈ 7.389
